<h2>From collaboration to complaint</h2>
<p>The dispute began as a property collaboration: a 2013 agreement for developing the petitioner\'s Ghaziabad property, with the complainant to construct the upper floors. On 1 February 2016, the parties recorded a handwritten settlement — exhibited as CW-1/Q — under which the complainant relinquished his claim to the second floor in exchange for Rs 30 lakh, payable through three cheques of Rs 10 lakh each. Two were dishonoured for insufficient funds; the third came back with the bank\'s endorsement "alteration in amount". The statutory demand notice of 27 June 2016 drew no payment, and the Metropolitan Magistrate convicted; the Additional Sessions Judge, Shahdara, affirmed.</p>

<h2>What a revision can and cannot do</h2>
<p>Justice Sanjeev Narula began where every cheque-bounce revision must: with the limits of the jurisdiction. Concurrent findings of fact are not reopened unless shown to be perverse or legally untenable, and none were. Within those limits, the Court tested each strand of the defence.</p>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>Security cheques?</strong> The admitted signatures and delivery triggered Sections 118(a) and 139. The rebuttal on offer was a bare explanation; the affidavit said to prove the security arrangement was never admitted or proved.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Set-off of Rs 25 lakh?</strong> Raised late, unsupported, and in any event incapable of displacing the settlement document, which an attesting witness proved independently.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Material alteration?</strong> The amount in words and figures matched; no unauthorised change was shown. Precedent on facially altered cheques was distinguished.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Section 311 recall?</strong> The refusal to recall witnesses caused no demonstrated prejudice, and the revisional court would not convert the plea into a retrial.</div>
</div>
<p>The Court also noted a point that practitioners under-use at their peril: the petitioner\'s silence in the face of the demand notice. A recipient who disputes liability is expected to say so when the statute gives him the occasion; silence then undermines the defence raised years later at trial.</p>

<h2>Conviction firm, sentence softened</h2>
<p>Having affirmed the conviction and the compensation of Rs 42 lakh, the Court turned to sentence with a different instrument. The transaction had its origin in a settlement; the petitioner had deposited Rs 21 lakh; and his family circumstances were pressed. Imprisonment was reduced from ten months to six, with the default sentence of two months undisturbed. Sums of Rs 12.6 lakh held with the Registry and Rs 8.4 lakh with the trial court were directed to the complainant, to be adjusted against the compensation.</p>
<div class="note">
<p>The structure of the outcome — conviction intact, sentence calibrated — reflects how Delhi courts currently balance the NI Act\'s deterrent purpose against its essentially compensatory character. The surest mitigation in a Section 138 matter remains money actually deposited or paid; rhetoric about hardship moves a court far less than a banker\'s receipt.</p>
</div>

<h2>The takeaway for drawers and payees</h2>
<p>For drawers of cheques, the case repeats three warnings: reply to the statutory notice, prove — not merely assert — any security-cheque arrangement, and raise set-off claims at the first opportunity with documents. For payees, it confirms that a settlement document proved through an attesting witness provides an independent foundation for liability beyond the presumptions. And for both sides, it illustrates that the revisional court is not a third trial: by that stage, the field of battle has narrowed to perversity and sentence.</p>
